      <article class="prose">
        <p>Shaped by tide, data, and saltwater, my career bridges enterprise, education, and environmental work. I'm currently the head of Product for the ocean NGO I co-founded, Ocean Agentics, building tools to help stakeholders of the UN BBNJ. I'm working on Deeptime, an AI system for coral annotation using Terabytes of photogrammetry data I've collected over the past 4 years in the southern Caribbean; I cofounded Dive the Data, a global sightings platform for the Marine Megafauna Foundation; I built enterprise SaaS for Morgan Stanley; and formed policy and communications with Shark Stewards in California. I'm an active research diver who has collected megafauna data in Mexico and Mozambique, studied urchin-kelp dynamics in California, and worked on coral reef disease with STINAPA in Bonaire. I'm a lifelong product designer, sailor, diver, and underwater videographer.</p>
        <p>I'm Dan Vallentyne. I work across marine research, software, and design, building tools that help people understand and care for the ocean.</p>
        <figure class="detail-hero">
          <img src="/assets/pb040217.webp" alt="Freediving above a sandy seabed in Tofo, Mozambique." width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Freediving in Tofo, Mozambique · 2018.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h2>How I got here</h2>
        <p>As a child, I studied tide-pool organisms and pored over books about animals, learning their names and how they fit together. I went on to study design, but my interest in ecology kept drawing me toward the ocean.</p>
        <p>One of my first studio jobs was at BAYCAT in San Francisco. I produced work for nonprofit and community organizations and taught graphic, web, and game design. From there, I worked on websites, applications, and educational experiences for studios, startups, and larger organizations.</p>
        <p>Alongside that work, I sailed, dived, and became involved in marine conservation. I earned my Divemaster qualification in Malaysian Borneo, worked as an underwater videographer on the Mesoamerican reef, and joined the Marine Megafauna Foundation in Mozambique as a research assistant and naturalist.</p>
        <p>In Mozambique, I helped collect field data, gave talks about marine life, and worked with local dive centers to develop Dive the Data, a citizen science project for recording and exploring marine megafauna sightings. It brought together many of the things I wanted to work on: ecology, public participation, and tools that make observations useful.</p>
        <h2>What I work on now</h2>
        <p>My marine work includes repeat reef surveys in Bonaire, underwater photogrammetry for Reef Check California, and surveys documenting marine debris beneath the Goto terminals. I've designed custom camera equipment and built workflows that turn underwater photographs into 3D reconstructions, reef maps, and data that researchers can inspect and compare over time.</p>
        <p>At Ocean Agentics, I lead product work and volunteer research teams developing ocean data tools. My projects include software for tracking funding opportunities, following High Seas Treaty events, and exploring connections between ocean data systems and organizations.</p>
        <p>I also contribute to scientific research. I'm the first author of a study on mercury in dried market fish and a coauthor of research on marine megafauna vulnerability to tuna fisheries.</p>
        <p>My design work continues to inform all of this. I've led research and interface design for enterprise applications at Morgan Stanley, developed educational experiences for Piper Computer, and created exhibits for the Pacific Grove Museum of Natural History. Across these settings, I start by understanding the people doing the work and what they need to make sense of.</p>
        <h2>What keeps me interested</h2>
        <p>I'm interested in ecological change, deep time, and how people participate in thriving ecosystems across generations. I want my work to connect scientific understanding with things people can do: collect better observations, understand a changing habitat, share knowledge, or make a conservation project possible.</p>
